WEBVTT

00:00:00.748 --> 00:00:02.593
Nonessential elements.

00:00:02.593 --> 00:00:08.507
Extra information gets a matching pair of marks: commas, dashes, or parentheses.

00:00:10.048 --> 00:00:11.913
Here's how to spot one.

00:00:11.913 --> 00:00:16.053
A comma or a dash already opens a phrase in the sentence.

00:00:16.053 --> 00:00:18.159
And the choices close it in different ways.

00:00:19.193 --> 00:00:23.405
It's testing whether you can see where extra information starts, and where it ends.

00:00:24.469 --> 00:00:25.954
Here's the method.

00:00:25.954 --> 00:00:28.164
Find the extra information.

00:00:28.164 --> 00:00:29.924
Delete it in your head.

00:00:29.924 --> 00:00:32.119
Is the sentence still complete?

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:34.029
Match the opening mark.

00:00:34.029 --> 00:00:36.434
A dash closes with a dash.

00:00:36.434 --> 00:00:38.679
A comma closes with a comma.

00:00:38.679 --> 00:00:41.263
And a title right before a name takes no commas.

00:00:42.343 --> 00:00:46.678
Rule one: extra information needs a matching pair of marks.

00:00:46.678 --> 00:00:50.003
Here a comma opens it, but nothing closes it.

00:00:50.003 --> 00:00:51.111
Add the second comma.

00:00:52.173 --> 00:00:55.338
Rule two: never mix a dash with a comma.

00:00:55.338 --> 00:00:59.193
Opened with a dash, closed with a comma, is wrong.

00:00:59.193 --> 00:01:00.626
Dash, then dash.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.725
Rule three: a title right before a name gets no commas.

00:01:05.725 --> 00:01:08.995
Not astronaut, comma, Sally Ride.

00:01:08.995 --> 00:01:10.728
Just astronaut Sally Ride.

00:01:11.781 --> 00:01:13.281
Let's try one.

00:01:13.281 --> 00:01:17.226
Here's the extra information, and it opens with a dash.

00:01:17.226 --> 00:01:21.751
If we delete it, we get: the seed vault stores backup copies.

00:01:21.751 --> 00:01:24.011
That's still a complete sentence.

00:01:24.011 --> 00:01:27.791
Because it opened with a dash, it must close with a dash.

00:01:27.791 --> 00:01:29.966
A comma would mix the marks.

00:01:29.966 --> 00:01:33.291
A semicolon would cut the subject off from its verb.

00:01:33.291 --> 00:01:35.716
And no mark at all would leave it open.

00:01:35.716 --> 00:01:36.848
The answer is B.

00:01:37.906 --> 00:01:40.276
Watch out for mismatched marks.

00:01:40.276 --> 00:01:43.296
Opened with a dash, closed with a comma.

00:01:43.296 --> 00:01:44.546
Always match the pair.

00:01:45.581 --> 00:01:52.241
Here's the whole lesson on one page: how to spot it, the four steps, the rules, and the trap.

00:01:52.241 --> 00:01:55.061
Pause the video now, and copy it into your notes.
